---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const planTripFAQ = [
  {
    q: "How many days should I allow for a traditional clothing trip?",
    a: "The constraint is costume time, not sightseeing. Five days lets you stay in one city and still book two full shoot days; a week adds a second city and one rail journey; two weeks is where you can fit tailoring, because a made-to-measure qipao or tang suit needs a fitting day and a collection day with travel in between. The itineraries page lays each length out day by day.",
  },
  {
    q: "What order should I make my planning decisions in?",
    a: "Season first, because it decides which cities are pleasant to be in and which weeks are unusable. Then the era you want to wear, which narrows the cities. Then the number of days that routing needs, and only then the flights — the rail legs, rentals and fittings can all be arranged after you know those three. Each of the planning guides on this page covers one of those steps.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I do this trip properly in one city?",
    a: "Yes, and for photography it is often the better call. One city deep means you learn the light, the shop owners and which backdrops are quiet at which hour, instead of spending the trip on station platforms. The experience map shows how dense a single city's offer is before you commit to a multi-city route.",
  },
];

const schemaPlanTripFAQ = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": planTripFAQ.map((faq) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    "name": faq.q,
    "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": faq.a },
  })),
});
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Plan a Traditional Chinese Clothing Trip"
  description="Plan a traditional clothing trip to China: entry rules, day-by-day itineraries, budgets, best months, rail travel and packing — one page per decision."
  canonicalUrl="/plan-trip/"
  articleDate="2026-10-20"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "Plan Your Trip", url: "/plan-trip/" }]}>
  <link slot="head" rel="preload" as="image" href="/images/plan-trip-hero.webp" fetchpriority="high" />
  <section class="pt-24 pb-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <span class="text-xs font-medium text-[#b5343a] tracking-[0.15em] uppercase">Travel Planning</span>
      <h1 class="font-serif-sc text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold leading-tight tracking-tight text-stone-900 mt-3">
        Plan Your Traditional Wear Journey
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-stone-600 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
        Everything you need to turn "I want to try Hanfu in China" into an actual trip with flights, trains, hotels, and booked experiences.
      </p>

      <!-- Hero image -->
      <div class="mt-8 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl aspect-[2/1] bg-stone-100">
        <img
          src="/images/plan-trip-hero.webp"
          alt="Travelers experiencing traditional Chinese clothing at a cultural workshop"
          class="w-full h-full object-cover"
          loading="eager"
          fetchpriority="high"
          width="1200" height="600"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </div>

      <!-- Quick jump nav -->
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        <a href="#visa" class="text-xs px-3 py-1.5 bg-stone-100 rounded-full text-stone-600 hover:bg-stone-200 transition-colors">Visa & Entry</a>
        <a href="#getting-around" class="text-xs px-3 py-1.5 bg-stone-100 rounded-full text-stone-600 hover:bg-stone-200 transition-colors">Getting Around</a>
        <a href="#itineraries" class="text-xs px-3 py-1.5 bg-stone-100 rounded-full text-stone-600 hover:bg-stone-200 transition-colors">Itineraries</a>
        <a href="#budget" class="text-xs px-3 py-1.5 bg-stone-100 rounded-full text-stone-600 hover:bg-stone-200 transition-colors">Budget</a>
        <a href="#seasons" class="text-xs px-3 py-1.5 bg-stone-100 rounded-full text-stone-600 hover:bg-stone-200 transition-colors">When to Go</a>
        <a href="#packing" class="text-xs px-3 py-1.5 bg-stone-100 rounded-full text-stone-600 hover:bg-stone-200 transition-colors">What to Pack</a>
      </div>

      <!-- ===== VISA ===== -->
      <h2 id="visa" class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-16 mb-4">Visa & Entry: Getting into China</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        <strong>30-day visa-free entry:</strong> Citizens of around 50 countries — most of Europe plus the UK, Canada and several Asian passports — may enter for tourism without a visa or a fee and stay up to 30 days. The published waiver runs through 31 December 2026. Check your passport against the current list on the <a href="https://en.nia.gov.cn/n147418/n147463/c183390/content.html" class="text-[#b5343a] underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Immigration Administration site</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        <strong>240-hour transit waiver:</strong> If your passport is not on that list, routing can solve it. Travellers from 57 countries may stay up to 10 days visa-free when they arrive from one country or region and depart to another, across 65 open ports including Beijing, Shanghai, Xi'an, Chengdu, Hangzhou, Nanjing and Guangzhou. A cheap onward hop to Hong Kong converts a round trip into a valid transit.
      </p>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        <strong>Tourist L visa:</strong> For longer stays and passports outside both waivers, apply at a Chinese Visa Application Service Center. Standard turnaround is about four business days, express two to three; fees are reciprocal and set by nationality. Have your passport, form, photo, flight and hotel bookings and a printed itinerary ready.
      </p>
      <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5 text-sm">
        <p class="text-stone-700"><strong>Full walkthrough:</strong> our <a href="/plan-trip/visa/" class="text-[#b5343a] underline font-medium">China entry and visa guide</a> covers which waiver fits your routing, what the border officer asks for, and how to present a cultural-tourism itinerary. Book refundable flights and hotels for any visa application, then adjust once it is approved.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- ===== PLAN IN DETAIL ===== -->
      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-16 mb-4">Plan It in Detail</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">Each part of the trip has its own page, with the numbers kept current and cross-checked before every season.</p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <a href="/plan-trip/visa/" class="block bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5 hover:border-[#b5343a]/40 hover:bg-white transition-colors"><h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 text-sm">Visa & entry rules</h3><p class="text-xs text-stone-600 mt-1.5 leading-relaxed">Which waiver applies to your passport and routing, and what to carry through immigration.</p></a>
        <a href="/plan-trip/seasons/" class="block bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5 hover:border-[#b5343a]/40 hover:bg-white transition-colors"><h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 text-sm">Best months to go</h3><p class="text-xs text-stone-600 mt-1.5 leading-relaxed">Weather, light and crowd patterns for photoshoots, city by city.</p></a>
        <a href="/plan-trip/itineraries/" class="block bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5 hover:border-[#b5343a]/40 hover:bg-white transition-colors"><h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 text-sm">Day-by-day itineraries</h3><p class="text-xs text-stone-600 mt-1.5 leading-relaxed">5, 7 and 14-day routes built around rental shops, tailors and shoot locations.</p></a>
        <a href="/plan-trip/budget/" class="block bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5 hover:border-[#b5343a]/40 hover:bg-white transition-colors"><h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 text-sm">What the trip costs</h3><p class="text-xs text-stone-600 mt-1.5 leading-relaxed">Rental, tailoring, rail and hotel ranges, plus how China compares with Japan and Korea.</p></a>
        <a href="/plan-trip/transport/" class="block bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5 hover:border-[#b5343a]/40 hover:bg-white transition-colors"><h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 text-sm">Getting between cities</h3><p class="text-xs text-stone-600 mt-1.5 leading-relaxed">Train times and fares, app setup, and how to buy a ticket as a first-timer.</p></a>
        <a href="/plan-trip/packing/" class="block bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5 hover:border-[#b5343a]/40 hover:bg-white transition-colors"><h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 text-sm">What to pack</h3><p class="text-xs text-stone-600 mt-1.5 leading-relaxed">Apps to install before you fly, and what to bring for a costume photoshoot.</p></a>
      </div>

      <!-- ===== GETTING AROUND ===== -->
      <h2 id="getting-around" class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-16 mb-4">Getting Around China</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        High-speed rail is the backbone of a traditional wear trip. It connects every city on our experience map, runs punctually, and makes an awkward pairing work: a morning fitting in Suzhou and an afternoon shoot in Hangzhou are realistic on the same day. Signage on the major routes is bilingual and station staff are used to travellers presenting paper passports.
      </p>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Inside the cities, the metro reaches nearly every rental shop, tailor street and shoot location we list. For the gaps, Didi and taxis are inexpensive — but an English hotel address often fails, so keep each destination written in Chinese characters on your phone before you get in.
      </p>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The transport guide carries the route-by-route table with times and fares, plus the first-timer walkthrough of buying a ticket, which app to use, and how far ahead tickets go on sale.
      </p>
      <a href="/plan-trip/transport/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-medium text-[#b5343a] underline underline-offset-4">See every route, fare and how to buy your first ticket →</a>

      <!-- ===== ITINERARIES ===== -->
      <h2 id="itineraries" class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-16 mb-4">Sample Itineraries</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Four tested routes, each built around costume time first and sightseeing second: a five-day Xi'an immersion that squeezes the most shoot days out of one city; a qipao and silk trail through Shanghai, Suzhou and Hangzhou; an ethnic textile trail through Guizhou and Yunnan; and a fourteen-day route that starts in Beijing, works through the eastern silk cities and finishes in Xi'an.
      </p>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Every day is named against a real rental shop, tailor street or shoot location, with the best months for that routing. Take a route whole, or lift segments from two and join them at a rail hub.
      </p>
      <a href="/plan-trip/itineraries/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-medium text-[#b5343a] underline underline-offset-4">Open the four day-by-day itineraries →</a>

      <!-- ===== BUDGET ===== -->
      <h2 id="budget" class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-16 mb-4">What the Trip Costs</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Two choices set the size of your budget: how many shoot days you book, and whether you buy made-to-measure pieces or rent them. Rail, food and hotel costs are low and fairly flat by comparison, and paying is almost entirely cashless once Alipay or WeChat Pay is linked to your card.
      </p>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The budget guide gives per-person tables for a seven-day and a fourteen-day trip at both a hostel tier and a comfortable tier, a side-by-side with Japan and South Korea, the line items first-timers miss — rental deposits, retouching fees, garden photography surcharges — and the booking habits that cut the total.
      </p>
      <a href="/plan-trip/budget/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-medium text-[#b5343a] underline underline-offset-4">Get the full cost breakdown and saving tips →</a>

      <!-- ===== SEASONS ===== -->
      <h2 id="seasons" class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-16 mb-4">When to Go</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Spring and autumn are the default answers, and for good reason: comfortable temperatures for full costume days, and light that flatters both silk and stone. The real planning constraint is the domestic holiday calendar — a route that is empty on a Tuesday in late April can be unusable during a national golden week the same month.
      </p>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The seasons page goes city by city: which months give the clearest skies and the best golden hour, where the blossom and foliage calendar puts you in front of the camera, how to work summer heat around a layered outfit, and which weeks to avoid entirely.
      </p>
      <a href="/plan-trip/seasons/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-medium text-[#b5343a] underline underline-offset-4">Compare months, city by city →</a>

      <!-- ===== PACKING ===== -->
      <h2 id="packing" class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-16 mb-4">What to Pack</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Two things trip people up: arriving with no payment app set up, and having no plan for what to wear under a rented costume. Everything else — shoes, layers, chargers — is either cheap to replace locally or supplied by the rental shop.
      </p>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The packing guide covers the apps to install and test before you fly, the clothing and gear that survives fifteen thousand steps a day at a heritage site, and the undergarment, makeup and reference-photo checklist to hand your stylist on shoot day.
      </p>
      <a href="/plan-trip/packing/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-medium text-[#b5343a] underline underline-offset-4">Open the pre-trip packing checklist →</a>

      <!-- ===== FAQ ===== -->
      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-16 mb-4">Planning Questions</h2>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <details class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5">
          <summary class="font-semibold text-sm text-stone-900 cursor-pointer">How many days should I allow for a traditional clothing trip?</summary>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 mt-2 leading-relaxed">The constraint is costume time, not sightseeing. Five days lets you stay in one city and still book two full shoot days; a week adds a second city and one rail journey; two weeks is where you can fit tailoring, because a made-to-measure qipao or tang suit needs a fitting day and a collection day with travel in between. The <a href="/plan-trip/itineraries/" class="text-[#b5343a] underline">itineraries</a> page lays each length out day by day.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5">
          <summary class="font-semibold text-sm text-stone-900 cursor-pointer">What order should I make my planning decisions in?</summary>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 mt-2 leading-relaxed">Season first, because it decides which cities are pleasant to be in and which weeks are unusable. Then the era you want to wear, which narrows the cities. Then the number of days that routing needs, and only then the flights — the rail legs, rentals and fittings can all be arranged after you know those three. Each of the <a href="/plan-trip/" class="text-[#b5343a] underline">planning guides</a> on this page covers one of those steps.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5">
          <summary class="font-semibold text-sm text-stone-900 cursor-pointer">Can I do this trip properly in one city?</summary>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 mt-2 leading-relaxed">Yes, and for photography it is often the better call. One city deep means you learn the light, the shop owners and which backdrops are quiet at which hour, instead of spending the trip on station platforms. The <a href="/#experience-map" class="text-[#b5343a] underline">experience map</a> shows how dense a single city's offer is before you commit to a multi-city route.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
      <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={schemaPlanTripFAQ}></script>

      <!-- CTA -->
      <div class="mt-16 bg-stone-900 rounded-2xl p-8 sm:p-10 text-center">
        <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Ready to Plan Your Hanfu Trip?</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-300 text-sm leading-relaxed max-w-lg mx-auto">Browse our <a href="/hanfu/" class="text-white underline hover:text-stone-200">Hanfu guide</a> for costume details, check <a href="/dynasties/" class="text-white underline hover:text-stone-200">dynasty styles</a> to pick your era, or explore <a href="/where-to-buy/" class="text-white underline hover:text-stone-200">where to buy</a> authentic pieces.</p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
